From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Subject: [PATCH] ntdll: add NtQueryInformationByName and FileStatLxInformation

Purpose: a Windows program learns the Unix mode, owner and group of a file
(XP Explorer's Linux columns, xp-explorer/helpers/linuxcols.c) without
opening it.
Windows behavior: Windows 10 (1709) has NtQueryInformationByName, which
asks about a file by its name, for FileStatInformation,
FileStatLxInformation and a few other classes. FileStatLxInformation
(class 70, FILE_STAT_LX_INFORMATION, which WSL uses) is
FileStatInformation's fields followed by the Unix mode, user, group and
device numbers.
Wine before: no NtQueryInformationByName, and FileStatLxInformation was
refused. No Win32 or NT call returned a file's mode or owner. Asking through
a handle would also make the wineserver open() the file: a request for every
file, side effects on some devices, and a hung mount holds up the
single-threaded wineserver, and with it every Windows program.
Change: NtQueryInformationByName answers the two classes from the Unix name
with lstat(): nothing is opened, and only a name relative to a directory
handle asks the wineserver. A symbolic link is reported itself, as a reparse
point (IO_REPARSE_TAG_LX_SYMLINK) with its own mode and owner; a link to a
directory also has FILE_ATTRIBUTE_DIRECTORY. FIFOs, sockets and devices are
answered. Other classes return STATUS_INVALID_INFO_CLASS.
NtQueryInformationFile takes FileStatLxInformation too, for any file type it
has a Unix fd for. The new system call has number 0x102; the 32-bit
NtWow64* calls after it move up by one.
Evidence: tests/stat-lx-probe.c compared with stat(1).
Scope: all programs; only adds what was refused or missing before.
